  "interpretation": "# How people used the Excel tools\n\n## What changed\n\nOver 90 days, 2,676 people took 1,172,860 actions, and 63.77% were repeat users. In the latest 14 days, people rose 23.79% to 640, while actions rose 110.07% to 232,064. Weekly people peaked at 830 in mid-August, fell to 323 in late September, and recovered to 422. Actions grew faster than people, which suggests a core group is doing more each.\n\nPeople upgrade quickly. Release 2.0.8 reached 56.28% of weekly people soon after launch, and 2.4.1 reached 51.29% in the latest week.\n\n## How people use it\n\nReading and writing cell values are the most common actions. Tables and ranges make up 63.34% of actions. Share of work is an estimate from fixed effort levels: quick reads count 1, everyday edits count 3, heavy data work counts 10. It is not time or speed. By that estimate, macros make up 8.11% of actions but 17.32% of work, and data import (Power Query) 3.96% of actions but 10.73% of work. Fewer people use these, but their use may be substantial.\n\nSince late September, 2,485 people used the AI assistant and 233 used the command line. These are different people over a shorter window. Command line users took 574.3 actions each, against 203.4, and put 75.98% of actions into tables and ranges, against 59.94%. Assistant users seem to lean more on macros, at 10.22% of actions against 1.03%.\n\nIn the last 30 days, 64.98% of assistant sessions used more than one area, most often cells with worksheets. Of 2,583 new people, 51.57% returned after a week. Among macro users, 79.32% first used it on day one. Typical waits ranged from 0.06 seconds for worksheets to 1.89 seconds for data import, but waits depend on workbook size and computer. Workdays averaged 27,108 actions, weekends 14,031.\n\n## Where people get it\n\nGitHub release files show 25,678 downloads, VS Code 9,106 installs, npm AI assistant server 5,854, and npm command line 3,772. Weekly npm downloads rose from 2,891 to 5,178. Release 2.4.1 was downloaded most, with 887, then 2.3.9 with 441. In the week from 12 October, downloads grew by 1,794, including 1,563 GitHub release files and 231 VS Code installs.\n\nDownloads count downloads, not people. One person can download several times, and updates and automated installs add to totals. Channels overlap, so they should not be added up. npm totals cover the last 12 months, and others count since each channel started.\n\n## What we will improve\n\nThese are plans, not findings. We will make heavy tasks like macros and data import easier to follow, help new people find a second area early, and keep frequent releases simple to install.",
  "interpretationModel": "GitHub Copilot CLI"
